          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose Them</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills. The list is built from manufacturers' published
            specifications and warranty terms, retailer listings and independent review coverage,
            all checked on 13 October 2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first filter was strict: a machine qualified only if its manufacturer's own product
            or specification page lists a negative grade. Reviews and retailer pages sometimes
            describe decline that the manufacturer does not, often because they describe an earlier
            model with the same name. We then checked each qualifying machine against a retailer
            listing and at least one independent review, and put only figures that agreed into the
            spec lines.
          </p>
          <p>
            That filter removed more machines than it kept. <strong>Horizon</strong>'s five current
            treadmills list 0 to 15% at most, with no decline. <strong>Bowflex</strong>'s current T6,
            T9 and T16 list incline up to 15% and no decline; the older Bowflex T10 and T22, which
            reviews describe with -5% decline, were not on Bowflex's site.
            <strong>Peloton</strong>'s current Tread Vision and Tread+ Vision specifications list
            incline from 0% and no decline. <strong>Sole</strong>'s TT8 lists a -6 to 15 grade range
            but was sold out on Sole's store; Sole's own blog also describes decline on the F85, but
            the F85's product page lists 0 to 15 incline levels and does not mention decline, so we
            did not list it. <strong>ProForm</strong> has sold Pro-series treadmills with -3%
            decline, but on the day we checked we could not load ProForm's own site to confirm
            which models are current, and the Carbon Pro models we could confirm at retail list 0 to
            12% incline only. Rather than list a ProForm on a review's word, we left the brand out
            of this edition.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for each
            brand and model. We found no open recall for any treadmill on this list.
          </p>
          <p>
            Among the qualifying machines, the ranking weighs the decline and incline range first,
            because they are the reason for the page; then the motor warranty, as the best signal
            of how long a manufacturer expects the machine to last under the extra load of hill
            work; then price, and whether the machine folds.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-decline-is-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Decline Is Actually For</h2>
<p>
            Most treadmill buyers never need decline. It is worth paying for when your training
            or your goal genuinely includes going downhill, and there are three common cases.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Downhill race preparation.</strong> Some road races lose a lot of height, either
            overall or in long early sections. Running downhill at pace is a skill and a specific
            stress: stride lengthens, impact rises and the muscles at the front of the thigh work
            hard to control each landing. Runners who only ever train on the flat or uphill can
            arrive at a downhill course with legs that are fit but not prepared for that load, and
            feel it badly in the second half. Regular, short decline sessions in the weeks before
            are one way to prepare.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Hiking and mountain trips.</strong> Hikers tend to train the climb and forget the
            descent, yet long descents are where tired legs, sore knees and slips tend to happen.
            Walking on a decline with a pack is a reasonable rehearsal for the second half of a day
            in the hills, and a machine that can also climb steeply lets you practise both halves of
            the same hill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Eccentric quad loading.</strong> Going downhill makes the quadriceps work
            eccentrically — lengthening under load as they brake each step — far more than flat or
            uphill movement does. Eccentric work is what leaves legs sore for days after a first
            hilly race, and it is also the type of muscle work the legs adapt to fastest when it is
            introduced gradually. That adaptation is a large part of why runners who include some
            downhill training tend to recover better from hilly races.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is also a simpler fourth use: realism. Outdoor-route workouts on iFIT and Zwift
            routes include descents, and a treadmill that can follow them makes those sessions feel
            more like the road. That is a nice-to-have rather than a reason to pay more. If your
            main interest is climbing, our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>
            guide covers machines built for that, most of which do not decline at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-decline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Much Decline: -3% vs -6%</h2>
<p>
            Every powered-decline machine we found offers either -3% or -6%. The difference sounds
            small on paper and is not small on the legs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the numbers mean.</strong> Treadmill grade is a percentage: rise or fall
            divided by distance. A -3% grade falls three feet for every hundred feet travelled,
            which is about 1.7 degrees; -6% is about 3.4 degrees. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-incline-percent-vs-degrees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill incline percent versus degrees</a>
            explains the conversion and why percentages read steeper than they are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>-3% is the road-runner's range.</strong> It is roughly the gradient of a gently
            falling road, and most downhill sections of road races sit around it. At -3% you can
            run at normal training pace, the change in muscle loading is clear, and it is easy to
            build up from. The NordicTrack Commercial series and the KICKR RUN all stop here, and
            for most runners preparing for a road race it is enough.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>-6% is the trail and hiking range.</strong> Double the grade and descents feel
            very different: braking forces rise, cadence tends to rise, and the session becomes a
            strength stimulus in its own right. Mountain trails, fell races and steep hiking routes
            regularly exceed this, so even -6% is a rehearsal, not a simulation. Only the NordicTrack
            X Series offered it among the machines we could confirm.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How to use either.</strong> Start with short decline segments inside an ordinary
            session — a few minutes at -1% to -2% — rather than a long continuous descent, and build
            over several weeks. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-hill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill hill workouts</a>
            guide sets out structured sessions; the decline versions follow the same logic of short
            repeats, building slowly, with recovery on the flat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="knees" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Decline and Your Knees</h2>
<p>
            Decline is the treadmill setting most likely to aggravate sore knees, and it is worth
            understanding why before you buy a machine for it. This section is general information,
            not medical advice; if you have a knee condition, a recent injury or pain that persists,
            talk to a doctor or physical therapist before adding downhill training.
          </p>
          <p>
            Going downhill shifts work towards the front of the knee. With each step the
            quadriceps brake the body's forward and downward motion, and the force through the
            kneecap joint is higher than on the flat. For healthy knees that is a training stimulus
            the body adapts to, and it is exactly why runners use decline to prepare for downhill
            courses. For knees that are already irritable at the front — the familiar ache around
            or behind the kneecap — the same loading can make things worse.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three practical points follow. First, introduce decline gradually and expect muscle
            soreness after the first sessions; that is normal, whereas sharp or joint pain is a
            reason to stop. Second, if you are buying a treadmill mainly to be kind to your knees,
            incline walking is generally the gentler direction, and decline is a feature you may
            never use. Third, shorter strides and a slightly quicker cadence reduce braking forces on
            any descent, on the treadmill or outdoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            Our guide to whether
            <a href="/are-treadmills-bad-for-your-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills are bad for your knees</a>
            covers the evidence on treadmill running and joint load more broadly, and our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for bad knees</a>
            roundup focuses on cushioning and low-impact use rather than decline.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="incline-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Other Half: Incline Range</h2>
<p>
            Every decline treadmill is also an incline treadmill, and the incline range often
            decides between them more than the decline does.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>12% (NordicTrack Commercial series).</strong> Twelve per cent is steep enough for
            hill repeats, incline walking and most race preparation. It is the ceiling on all three
            Commercial models, so paying more within the series buys screen and speed, not climbing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>15% (Wahoo KICKR RUN).</strong> A little more headroom for steep walking and
            hill intervals, and the range Zwift uses when it controls the deck.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>40% (NordicTrack X16).</strong> An incline trainer's range. Steep walking at
            20% and above is a very different workout from running: high effort at low speed and
            low impact. If that appeals as much as the decline does, the X16 covers both ends of a
            mountain in one machine. If you are unsure whether you need an incline trainer, our
            <a href="/incline-trainer-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline trainer vs treadmill</a>
            guide sets out the trade-offs.
          </p>
          <p>
            A useful way to choose: work out the steepest climb and the steepest descent your goal
            involves, and pick the machine whose range covers the more demanding of the two. For a
            road runner that is usually a Commercial-series machine; for a hiker, usually the X16.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="app-question" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Does Decline Need an App?</h2>
<p>
            Not for the hardware to work — but the app changes how you use decline, and on the
            NordicTrack machines it is worth confirming one detail before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack and iFIT.</strong> Independent reviews describe NordicTrack
            treadmills running in a manual mode without a paid iFIT membership, in which you set
            speed and incline yourself, and retailer listings describe grade controls that step in
            half-per-cent increments. What we could not find is a NordicTrack statement that the
            negative settings are available in manual mode on the current software, so if you
            intend to use decline without paying for iFIT, ask NordicTrack or the retailer to
            confirm it for the exact model before you order. What the membership certainly adds is
            automatic control: trainer-led workouts and outdoor-route videos move the deck up and
            down for you, including into decline on the descents. For structured downhill training
            that is genuinely useful, because changing grade by hand mid-run is a distraction. It
            is also an ongoing cost, and our
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">iFIT guide</a> explains
            what the membership includes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wahoo and open apps.</strong> Wahoo says the KICKR RUN needs no subscription, that
            you can set the grade manually and that automatic adjustment can be switched off. Zwift
            can control its grade, including decline, on routes; Zwift is a separate service with
            its own fee. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-zwift/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift treadmill guide</a>
            explains which treadmills Zwift can and cannot control.
          </p>
          <p>
            In short: buy for the decline range and the hardware, and treat the app as a choice
            about how much of the work of changing grade you want to hand over.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Skip Decline</h2>
<p>
            <strong>If you will mostly walk or run on the flat.</strong> Decline adds cost and is
            only available on machines that start around $1,600. Without a downhill goal, the money
            is better spent on a better deck or a longer warranty; our
            <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline treadmill roundup</a>
            covers machines that climb well for less.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you never want a subscription.</strong> Four of the five machines here are
            built around iFIT, and the fifth costs several times as much. If you would rather
            avoid platform-led machines altogether, our
            <a href="/best-horizon-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon roundup</a>
            covers a range with no platform at all — and no decline.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your knees are already sore at the front.</strong> Decline loads the front of
            the knee more than flat or uphill work. Get the knee assessed first, and choose a
            machine for incline and cushioning instead.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If space is tight.</strong> The X16 does not fold and needs considerable ceiling
            height. The Commercial-series machines fold, but they are large and heavy treadmills
            either way. In a small room, decline is usually the first feature to give up.
          </p>
        </div>

