        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmill has the most decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among the home treadmills we could confirm on 13 October 2026, the NordicTrack X16 and X24 go furthest, to -6%, with incline up to 40%. The NordicTrack Commercial 1250, 1750 and 2450 and the Wahoo KICKR RUN decline to -3%. Sole lists a -6 to 15 grade range on its TT8, but it was sold out on the day we checked.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is -3% decline enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most runners, yes. Minus three per cent is roughly the gradient of a gently falling road and covers the downhill sections of most road races. It is enough to train the braking work of the quadriceps. Hikers and trail runners preparing for long, steep descents will get more from the -6% on the NordicTrack X Series.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is running on a decline bad for your knees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Decline increases the load on the front of the knee because the quadriceps brake each step. Healthy knees adapt to it when it is introduced gradually, which is why runners use it. Knees that are already sore at the front can be aggravated. This is general information, not medical advice; see a professional if you have knee pain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need an iFIT subscription to use decline on a NordicTrack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not to use the treadmill: reviews describe a manual mode without a paid membership, in which you set speed and incline yourself. We could not find a NordicTrack statement confirming the decline settings are available in manual mode on current software, so ask before ordering if that matters. The membership adds workouts that move the deck automatically, including into decline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do any cheap treadmills have decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not among the current models we could confirm. The least expensive machine with manufacturer-confirmed powered decline was the NordicTrack Commercial 1250, at around $1,600 to $1,800. Some older models with decline still appear at third-party sellers, but check that the decline is confirmed for the exact model year before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is -3% decline in degrees?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 1.7 degrees. Treadmill grade is a percentage of rise or fall over distance, so -3% means the deck falls three feet for every hundred feet travelled. Minus six per cent is about 3.4 degrees. Percentages always read steeper than the equivalent number of degrees, which surprises many buyers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
