        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Decline Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most of these come from paying for decline without a clear use for it, or trusting a decline figure the manufacturer does not publish.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a review’s decline figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Model names persist while hardware changes, and reviews and blogs sometimes describe decline the current model does not have. Check the manufacturer’s own specification page for a negative grade before you pay extra for it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for decline you will not use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Without a downhill race, a hiking goal or route workouts you will follow, decline is a feature you will try twice. The same money buys a wider deck, a stronger warranty or a machine that folds more easily.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Starting with long descents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Decline loads the quadriceps eccentrically, and the first sessions cause more soreness than the effort suggests. Begin with a few minutes at a gentle grade inside a normal session and build over weeks.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the incline end of the range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The climb usually matters as much as the descent. A Commercial-series machine stops at 12% and the X16 reaches 40%; choose the range that covers the steepest part of your goal in both directions.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

